WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.912
Step four runs Microsoft's pinned
sample with the current module.

2
00:00:03.912 --> 00:00:08.466
Microsoft's sample, from the
Azure Policy samples repository,

3
00:00:08.466 --> 00:00:10.966
is pinned at an August 2021 commit,

4
00:00:10.966 --> 00:00:16.501
five years older than GuestConfiguration
4.12.0 on the PowerShell Gallery.

5
00:00:16.501 --> 00:00:20.261
Its MOF still packages, but
the cmdlet its script calls,

6
00:00:20.261 --> 00:00:25.025
test guest configuration package,
returns nothing: it's been renamed.

7
00:00:25.025 --> 00:00:29.113
Then check Microsoft's published
MOF with the kit's MOF check.

8
00:00:29.113 --> 00:00:32.623
It returns nothing, because
Microsoft's published MOF

9
00:00:32.623 --> 00:00:36.288
carries no build-host data,
the form the cleaner produces.

10
00:00:36.288 --> 00:00:38.917
That check needs none
of the gallery modules,

11
00:00:38.917 --> 00:00:41.688
and it printed nothing
again on November third.

12
00:00:41.688 --> 00:00:43.826
Step five runs the checked build.

13
00:00:43.826 --> 00:00:46.998
It exits zero, with one information row,

14
00:00:46.998 --> 00:00:51.362
MC-POL oh nine: include Arc
machines defaults to false.

15
00:00:51.362 --> 00:00:55.287
Build again into a second folder,
and compare the two manifests.

16
00:00:55.287 --> 00:00:59.906
The MOF hashes match, starting
e c f f d seven nine five,

17
00:00:59.906 --> 00:01:02.376
and the package hashes don't.

18
00:01:02.376 --> 00:01:05.280
So you publish the exact
bytes the build checked.
